insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0379$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Stoichiometric Calculations — Gas Volumes$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Stoichiometric Calculations — Gas Volumes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "v4o1jb", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$medium$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "22.4 dm3 is the volume of just 1 mole of gas \u2014 this treats the mole ratio as 1:1 (2 mol KClO3 giving only 1 mol O2), ignoring the balanced equation's actual 2:3 ratio.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "33.6 dm3 corresponds to 1.5 mol of O2, which would be correct if only 1 mole of KClO3 had decomposed \u2014 this misses that the question specifies 2 moles of KClO3, not 1.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "44.8 dm3 corresponds to 2 mol of O2, treating the KClO3:O2 ratio as 1:1 rather than the correct 2:3 from the balanced equation.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What volume of oxygen is produced from the decomposition of 2 moles of KClO3 at s.t.p? [Molar volume of a gas at s.t.p = 22.4 dm3]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "22.4dm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "33.6 dm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "44.8 dm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "67.2dm3", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$2KClO3 → 2KCl + 3O2. The mole ratio of KClO3 to O2 is 2:3, so 2 moles of KClO3 produce 3 moles of O2. Volume = 3 × 22.4 dm3 = 67.2 dm3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 22.4 dm3 is the volume of just 1 mole of gas — this treats the mole ratio as 1:1 (2 mol KClO3 giving only 1 mol O2), ignoring the balanced equation's actual 2:3 ratio. B: 33.6 dm3 corresponds to 1.5 mol of O2, which would be correct if only 1 mole of KClO3 had decomposed — this misses that the question specifies 2 moles of KClO3, not 1. C: 44.8 dm3 corresponds to 2 mol of O2, treating the KClO3:O2 ratio as 1:1 rather than the correct 2:3 from the balanced equation.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0380$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Physical vs Chemical Change$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Physical vs Chemical Change.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vvwi6v", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Burning kerosene is combustion \u2014 a chemical reaction that produces new substances (CO2, H2O, etc.), not a mere change of state.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "White phosphorus spontaneously ignites/oxidises on exposure to air, forming phosphorus oxides \u2014 a chemical reaction, not a physical change.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Calcium reacting with water produces calcium hydroxide and hydrogen gas \u2014 entirely new substances form, marking this as a chemical, not physical, change.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following is a physical change?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Burning kerosene", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Freezing ice-cream", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Exposing white phosphorus to air", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Dissolving calcium in water", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Freezing ice-cream is simply a change of state (liquid to solid) — no new substance forms, and it's reversible by melting. Burning kerosene (combustion), white phosphorus igniting in air, and calcium reacting with water are all chemical changes that produce entirely new substances.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Burning kerosene is combustion — a chemical reaction that produces new substances (CO2, H2O, etc.), not a mere change of state. C: White phosphorus spontaneously ignites/oxidises on exposure to air, forming phosphorus oxides — a chemical reaction, not a physical change. D: Calcium reacting with water produces calcium hydroxide and hydrogen gas — entirely new substances form, marking this as a chemical, not physical, change.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0381$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Percentage Composition$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Percentage Composition.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "naed66", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "14.29% is far too low \u2014 this doesn't account for the full 14 oxygen atoms present across both the sulfate groups and the water of crystallization.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "25.39% undercounts the oxygen contribution \u2014 possibly counting only the water's oxygens or only part of the sulfate oxygens rather than all 14.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "50.79% is closer but still short of the correct value \u2014 a likely small error in either the total molar mass or the oxygen atom count used in the division.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What is the percentage by mass of oxygen in Al2(SO4)3.2H2O? [Al = 27, S = 32, H = 1, O = 16]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "14.29%", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "25.39%", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "50.79%", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "59.25%", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Molar mass: Al2(SO4)3 = 2(27) + 3(32+64) = 54 + 288 = 342. Add 2H2O = 2(18) = 36. Total = 378 g/mol. Oxygen atoms: 12 from the three sulfate groups + 2 from the two water molecules = 14 oxygen atoms, mass = 14×16 = 224 g. Percentage O = 224/378 × 100 ≈ 59.25%.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 14.29% is far too low — this doesn't account for the full 14 oxygen atoms present across both the sulfate groups and the water of crystallization. B: 25.39% undercounts the oxygen contribution — possibly counting only the water's oxygens or only part of the sulfate oxygens rather than all 14. C: 50.79% is closer but still short of the correct value — a likely small error in either the total molar mass or the oxygen atom count used in the division.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0382$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Adsorption$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Adsorption.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "5jews9", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Burning describes combustion of the tobacco itself, not what the filter does to trap nicotine afterward \u2014 the filter's mechanism isn't about burning anything.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Evaporation would mean the nicotine turns to vapour and escapes \u2014 the filter's job is the opposite, trapping the nicotine rather than letting it evaporate away.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Absorption means being taken up into the bulk/interior of a substance, but filter trapping happens at the surface of the fibres \u2014 the correct, more precise term for this surface phenomenon is adsorption, not absorption.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The filter in a cigarette reduces the nicotine content by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "burning", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "adsorption", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "evaporation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "absorption", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Cigarette filters (often containing activated carbon or cellulose acetate fibres) trap nicotine molecules on their surface — this surface-trapping process is specifically called adsorption, distinct from absorption (which means being taken up into the bulk of a material).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Burning describes combustion of the tobacco itself, not what the filter does to trap nicotine afterward — the filter's mechanism isn't about burning anything. C: Evaporation would mean the nicotine turns to vapour and escapes — the filter's job is the opposite, trapping the nicotine rather than letting it evaporate away. D: Absorption means being taken up into the bulk/interior of a substance, but filter trapping happens at the surface of the fibres — the correct, more precise term for this surface phenomenon is adsorption, not absorption.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0383$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Combination$q$, $q$Balancing Chemical Equations — Atom Counting$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Balancing Chemical Equations — Atom Counting.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ej7de5", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "With p=1, there would only be 1 hydrogen atom on the left, but the right side needs 8 (from 4H2O) \u2014 this badly under-balances hydrogen.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "p=2 only supplies 2 H atoms, still far short of the 8 needed to balance the 4H2O on the product side.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "p=6 supplies 6 H atoms, still short of the 8 required by 4H2O \u2014 close, but not fully balanced; a small miscount in the hydrogen-balancing step.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$3Cu + pHNO3 → 3Cu(NO3)2 + 4H2O + xNO. In the equation above, the values of p and x respectively are$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "1 and 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "2 and 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "6 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "8 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Balance hydrogen first: the right side has 4H2O = 8 H atoms, so p (HNO3 coefficient) must be 8 to supply 8 H atoms. Check nitrogen: left side has p = 8 N atoms; right side has 3×2 = 6 N atoms in Cu(NO3)2 plus x in NO, so 8 = 6 + x, giving x = 2. Both values check out: p = 8, x = 2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: With p=1, there would only be 1 hydrogen atom on the left, but the right side needs 8 (from 4H2O) — this badly under-balances hydrogen. B: p=2 only supplies 2 H atoms, still far short of the 8 needed to balance the 4H2O on the product side. C: p=6 supplies 6 H atoms, still short of the 8 required by 4H2O — close, but not fully balanced; a small miscount in the hydrogen-balancing step.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0384$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Electronic Configuration and Periodic Blocks$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Electronic Configuration and Periodic Blocks.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "7u41s5", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Noble gas atoms don't have any particular molecular 'shape' at all \u2014 they exist as individual atoms, not as ring-shaped or cyclic structures.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Like 'cyclic shape', this invents a geometric explanation that doesn't apply \u2014 single atoms don't have hexagonal shapes; the real explanation is purely about electron configuration.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'Obtuse configuration' isn't a real chemistry term relevant to electron arrangement \u2014 this appears to be a plausible-sounding but meaningless distractor.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The noble gases owe their inactivity to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "octet configuration", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "cyclic shape", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "hexagonal shape", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "obtuse configuration", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Noble gases (except helium) have a complete octet of 8 electrons in their outermost shell, an especially stable arrangement that gives them very little tendency to gain, lose, or share electrons — hence their chemical inactivity.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Noble gas atoms don't have any particular molecular 'shape' at all — they exist as individual atoms, not as ring-shaped or cyclic structures. C: Like 'cyclic shape', this invents a geometric explanation that doesn't apply — single atoms don't have hexagonal shapes; the real explanation is purely about electron configuration. D: 'Obtuse configuration' isn't a real chemistry term relevant to electron arrangement — this appears to be a plausible-sounding but meaningless distractor.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0385$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Kinetic Theory & Gas Laws$q$, $q$Kinetic Theory — Temperature and Energy$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Kinetic Theory — Temperature and Energy.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "v89sra", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This reverses the actual relationship \u2014 kinetic energy rises with temperature, it doesn't fall.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Kinetic theory's central claim is precisely that kinetic energy CHANGES with temperature \u2014 treating it as constant misses the whole point of the theory.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Kinetic energy would only be zero at absolute zero (0 K) \u2014 an increase in temperature moves particles further from that state, not toward it.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$According to the kinetic theory, an increase in temperature causes the kinetic energy of particles to$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "remain constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "be zero", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Kinetic theory directly links temperature to the average kinetic energy of particles — as temperature rises, particles move faster on average, meaning their average kinetic energy increases.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This reverses the actual relationship — kinetic energy rises with temperature, it doesn't fall. C: Kinetic theory's central claim is precisely that kinetic energy CHANGES with temperature — treating it as constant misses the whole point of the theory. D: Kinetic energy would only be zero at absolute zero (0 K) — an increase in temperature moves particles further from that state, not toward it.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0386$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Kinetic Theory & Gas Laws$q$, $q$Kinetic Theory — Temperature and Energy$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Kinetic Theory — Temperature and Energy.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "v89sra", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Molecules do move at different speeds, but that fact alone doesn't explain what causes pressure \u2014 pressure specifically comes from wall collisions, not from speed variation among molecules.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Molecule-molecule collisions happen constantly inside the gas, but they don't create pressure on the container \u2014 it's specifically collisions with the walls that generate the outward force we call pressure.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Molecular mass affects HOW MUCH pressure results from a collision, but mass alone (without collisions happening) doesn't create pressure \u2014 this names a contributing factor rather than the actual mechanism.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A gas exerts pressure on its container because$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "some of its molecules are moving faster than others", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "of the collision of the molecules with each other", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "of the mass of the molecules of gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "the molecules of a gas collide with the walls of the container", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Gas pressure arises specifically from gas molecules continually colliding with and rebounding off the container walls, transferring momentum at each collision. Molecule-to-molecule collisions, varying speeds, and molecular mass alone don't directly explain what pressure IS — they're related but distinct aspects of kinetic theory.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Molecules do move at different speeds, but that fact alone doesn't explain what causes pressure — pressure specifically comes from wall collisions, not from speed variation among molecules. B: Molecule-molecule collisions happen constantly inside the gas, but they don't create pressure on the container — it's specifically collisions with the walls that generate the outward force we call pressure. C: Molecular mass affects HOW MUCH pressure results from a collision, but mass alone (without collisions happening) doesn't create pressure — this names a contributing factor rather than the actual mechanism.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0387$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$History of Atomic Theory$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on History of Atomic Theory.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "nucy3t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Cathode rays are electrons, which carry negative charge \u2014 depositing them onto the electrode would make it negative, not positive; this has the charge sign backwards.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The electrode gains extra negative charge from the accumulating electrons \u2014 it does not stay neutral, since charge is actively being deposited onto it.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'Bipolar' isn't a meaningful description of a simple charge accumulation \u2014 the electrode simply gains one type of charge (negative), not two opposing types simultaneously.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When cathode rays are deflected onto the electrode of an electrometer, the instrument becomes$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "negatively charged", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "positively charged", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "bipolar", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Cathode rays are streams of electrons — negatively charged particles. When they strike and accumulate on the electrometer's electrode, they deposit negative charge onto it, making the instrument negatively charged.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Cathode rays are electrons, which carry negative charge — depositing them onto the electrode would make it negative, not positive; this has the charge sign backwards. C: The electrode gains extra negative charge from the accumulating electrons — it does not stay neutral, since charge is actively being deposited onto it. D: 'Bipolar' isn't a meaningful description of a simple charge accumulation — the electrode simply gains one type of charge (negative), not two opposing types simultaneously.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0388$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Intermolecular Forces — Van der Waals$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Intermolecular Forces — Van der Waals.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "zxcqb1", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Ionic bonding is a strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions \u2014 one of the strongest bond types, not the weakest intermolecular force.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Covalent bonds are strong intramolecular bonds formed by shared electron pairs \u2014 far stronger than the weak forces that exist between separate molecules.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Coordinate covalent (dative) bonds are still a strong type of bonding where one atom supplies both shared electrons \u2014 this is a bond within/between atoms, not the weak attraction between whole molecules being asked about.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The weakest attractive forces that can be observed between two molecules is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "ionic", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "covalent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "coordinate covalent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Van der Waals", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Van der Waals forces are weak, temporary attractions arising from fluctuating electron distributions between molecules — much weaker than the strong chemical bonds (ionic, covalent, coordinate covalent) that hold atoms together within a molecule or compound.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Ionic bonding is a strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions — one of the strongest bond types, not the weakest intermolecular force. B: Covalent bonds are strong intramolecular bonds formed by shared electron pairs — far stronger than the weak forces that exist between separate molecules. C: Coordinate covalent (dative) bonds are still a strong type of bonding where one atom supplies both shared electrons — this is a bond within/between atoms, not the weak attraction between whole molecules being asked about.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0389$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Environmental Pollution$q$, $q$Greenhouse Gases$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Greenhouse Gases.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "jiwfyk", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Air pollution is more typically a CAUSE contributing to global warming (via greenhouse gases), not a consequence resulting from it \u2014 the causal direction here is reversed.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Water pollution isn't a direct, defining consequence of rising global temperatures in the way flooding is \u2014 it's a separate environmental issue with its own distinct causes.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Increased humidity can be a secondary/regional effect in some contexts, but it isn't the standard, most direct and widely-cited consequence of global warming compared to flooding from rising sea levels.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A consequence of global warming is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "air pollution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "water pollution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "increased humidity", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "flooding", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Global warming raises sea levels (through thermal expansion and melting polar ice) and intensifies extreme weather, both of which directly increase the risk and severity of flooding — a well-established and commonly cited direct consequence.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Air pollution is more typically a CAUSE contributing to global warming (via greenhouse gases), not a consequence resulting from it — the causal direction here is reversed. B: Water pollution isn't a direct, defining consequence of rising global temperatures in the way flooding is — it's a separate environmental issue with its own distinct causes. C: Increased humidity can be a secondary/regional effect in some contexts, but it isn't the standard, most direct and widely-cited consequence of global warming compared to flooding from rising sea levels.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0390$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acids, Bases & Salts$q$, $q$Acidic and Basic Ions — Salt Hydrolysis$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Acidic and Basic Ions — Salt Hydrolysis.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "yzd6cv", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "K+ is a spectator ion from a strong base (KOH) \u2014 it doesn't hydrolyse or affect solution pH, making it neutral, not acidic.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "NO3- is the conjugate base of a strong acid (HNO3), which means it's too weak a base to affect pH \u2014 it behaves as a neutral spectator ion, not an acidic one.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "S2- is actually basic \u2014 as the conjugate base of the weak acid H2S, it can accept protons from water, making solutions containing it alkaline rather than acidic.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following ions is acidic?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "K+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "NO3-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "S2-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "H3O+", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$H3O+ (the hydronium ion) is, by definition, the species responsible for acidity in aqueous solution — its presence and concentration directly determine how acidic a solution is. K+ and NO3- are spectator ions from a neutral salt (KNO3), and S2- is actually basic (it's the conjugate base of a weak acid, H2S).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: K+ is a spectator ion from a strong base (KOH) — it doesn't hydrolyse or affect solution pH, making it neutral, not acidic. B: NO3- is the conjugate base of a strong acid (HNO3), which means it's too weak a base to affect pH — it behaves as a neutral spectator ion, not an acidic one. C: S2- is actually basic — as the conjugate base of the weak acid H2S, it can accept protons from water, making solutions containing it alkaline rather than acidic.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0391$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemistry & Industry$q$, $q$Soap and Detergents — Structure and Function$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Soap and Detergents — Structure and Function.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gqm7dj", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "-COO-Na+ is the carboxylate group characteristic of ordinary SOAP, not detergent \u2014 this is the group that forms insoluble scum with hard-water ions, the opposite of the fast-dissolving detergent property being asked about.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "-SO4-Na+ (a sulfate, not sulfonate) is a plausible-sounding but structurally different group from the actual detergent-defining sulfonate (-SO3-Na+) \u2014 a subtle but real distinction between sulfate and sulfonate groups.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "-COO-K+ is still a carboxylate (soap-type) group, just with potassium instead of sodium \u2014 swapping the metal ion doesn't change that it's the soap-characteristic group, not the detergent one.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The structural component that makes detergent dissolve more quickly in water than soap is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "-SO3-Na+", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "-COO-Na+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "-SO4-Na+", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "-COO-K+", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Synthetic detergents typically use a sulfonate head group (-SO3-Na+), which stays fully soluble even in hard water (doesn't form insoluble precipitates with Ca2+/Mg2+ the way soap's carboxylate group does), giving detergents faster, more reliable dissolution and cleaning action. Soap's characteristic group is the carboxylate (-COO-Na+ or -COO-K+), which is the group that forms scum in hard water.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: -COO-Na+ is the carboxylate group characteristic of ordinary SOAP, not detergent — this is the group that forms insoluble scum with hard-water ions, the opposite of the fast-dissolving detergent property being asked about. C: -SO4-Na+ (a sulfate, not sulfonate) is a plausible-sounding but structurally different group from the actual detergent-defining sulfonate (-SO3-Na+) — a subtle but real distinction between sulfate and sulfonate groups. D: -COO-K+ is still a carboxylate (soap-type) group, just with potassium instead of sodium — swapping the metal ion doesn't change that it's the soap-characteristic group, not the detergent one.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0392$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Nature of Matter$q$, $q$Solubility of Fats — Polarity$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Solubility of Fats — Polarity.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "zz7t6m", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Hydrochloric acid is an aqueous, polar solution \u2014 fats (non-polar) don't dissolve appreciably in polar aqueous solutions.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Calcium hydroxide solution is also aqueous and polar \u2014 it won't dissolve non-polar fat molecules any more than plain water would, aside from any separate saponification reaction (which is a chemical breakdown, not simple dissolving).", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Water is the classic polar solvent \u2014 fats are famously insoluble in water ('oil and water don't mix'), which is exactly why a non-polar solvent like kerosene is needed instead.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A liquid that will dissolve fat is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "hydrochloric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "calcium hydroxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "kerosene", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "water", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Fats are non-polar substances, so 'like dissolves like' — they dissolve readily in non-polar organic solvents such as kerosene, but not in polar substances like water, dilute HCl, or aqueous calcium hydroxide.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Hydrochloric acid is an aqueous, polar solution — fats (non-polar) don't dissolve appreciably in polar aqueous solutions. B: Calcium hydroxide solution is also aqueous and polar — it won't dissolve non-polar fat molecules any more than plain water would, aside from any separate saponification reaction (which is a chemical breakdown, not simple dissolving). D: Water is the classic polar solvent — fats are famously insoluble in water ('oil and water don't mix'), which is exactly why a non-polar solvent like kerosene is needed instead.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0393$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Environmental Pollution$q$, $q$Oil Spillage Remediation$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Oil Spillage Remediation.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gntpzm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$recall$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Adding acid doesn't break down oil and could introduce further chemical damage to the soil rather than removing the contamination.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Water alone doesn't dissolve or remove oil (oil is non-polar and doesn't mix with water) \u2014 this wouldn't meaningfully decontaminate the affected soil.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Burning off the oil creates air pollution and can further damage the soil (e.g. through heat and residue) rather than genuinely remediating the contamination \u2014 trading one environmental problem for another.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Farmlands affected by crude-oil spillage can be decontaminated by$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "adding acidic solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "using aerobic bacteria", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "pouring water on the affected area", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "burning off the oil from the area", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Bioremediation — using aerobic bacteria that metabolise hydrocarbons — is the standard method for decontaminating oil-affected soil, breaking the oil down into less harmful substances without the further environmental damage caused by burning or simply adding water/acid.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Adding acid doesn't break down oil and could introduce further chemical damage to the soil rather than removing the contamination. C: Water alone doesn't dissolve or remove oil (oil is non-polar and doesn't mix with water) — this wouldn't meaningfully decontaminate the affected soil. D: Burning off the oil creates air pollution and can further damage the soil (e.g. through heat and residue) rather than genuinely remediating the contamination — trading one environmental problem for another.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0394$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solubility$q$, $q$Solubility and Saturation$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Solubility and Saturation.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "mu3oy8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "'Dissolved solute and the solvent' doesn't correctly name the two sides of the relevant equilibrium \u2014 the balance in a saturated solution is specifically between dissolved and undissolved solute, not between solute and solvent as separate entities.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This mismatches solvent (a bulk substance) against 'undissolved solute' \u2014 the actual equilibrium is between the two states of the SAME solute (dissolved vs undissolved), not between solvent and solute.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'The solution' as a whole isn't one side of this equilibrium \u2014 the dissolved solute IS already part of the solution; the real balance is against the separate undissolved solid phase.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A change in the temperature of a saturated solution disturbs the equilibrium between the$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "dissolved solute and the solvent", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "solvent and the undissolved solute", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "dissolved solute and the undissolved solute", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "dissolved solute and the solution", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$In a saturated solution, there's a dynamic equilibrium between solute already dissolved and any excess (undissolved) solute — the rate of dissolving equals the rate of recrystallising. Changing temperature shifts where that balance sits, since solubility itself is temperature-dependent, disturbing precisely this dissolved/undissolved equilibrium.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: 'Dissolved solute and the solvent' doesn't correctly name the two sides of the relevant equilibrium — the balance in a saturated solution is specifically between dissolved and undissolved solute, not between solute and solvent as separate entities. B: This mismatches solvent (a bulk substance) against 'undissolved solute' — the actual equilibrium is between the two states of the SAME solute (dissolved vs undissolved), not between solvent and solute. D: 'The solution' as a whole isn't one side of this equilibrium — the dissolved solute IS already part of the solution; the real balance is against the separate undissolved solid phase.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0395$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$moderate$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Temperature specifically matters for shifting an equilibrium with a nonzero \u0394H \u2014 claiming the shift happens at 'any temperature' ignores that Le Chatelier's principle predicts a direction-dependent response to temperature change.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Low temperature would favour the exothermic (backward) direction for this reaction, not the forward endothermic direction \u2014 this has the temperature effect backwards for an endothermic process.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'Minimum temperature' isn't a meaningful description of how equilibrium position responds to heat \u2014 the forward endothermic reaction is favoured by adding more heat (raising temperature), not by minimising it.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$If an equilibrium reaction has ΔH > 0, the reaction will proceed favourably in the forward direction at$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "high temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "any temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "low temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "minimum temperature", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$ΔH > 0 means the forward reaction is endothermic (absorbs heat). By Le Chatelier's principle, adding heat (raising temperature) shifts an endothermic equilibrium further forward, since the system responds to the added heat by consuming it via the forward reaction.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Temperature specifically matters for shifting an equilibrium with a nonzero ΔH — claiming the shift happens at 'any temperature' ignores that Le Chatelier's principle predicts a direction-dependent response to temperature change. C: Low temperature would favour the exothermic (backward) direction for this reaction, not the forward endothermic direction — this has the temperature effect backwards for an endothermic process. D: 'Minimum temperature' isn't a meaningful description of how equilibrium position responds to heat — the forward endothermic reaction is favoured by adding more heat (raising temperature), not by minimising it.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0396$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrolysis$q$, $q$Electrode Reactions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Electrode Reactions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "23bs2u", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Electrons are actually RELEASED (produced) at the anode during oxidation, not consumed \u2014 it's the cathode where electrons are consumed (in reduction).", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Reduction (gaining electrons) happens at the cathode, not the anode \u2014 the anode's defining feature is oxidation (losing electrons), the opposite process.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "The electrode dissolving is only true for specific cases (like an active/sacrificial anode), not a universal feature of anode reactions in general \u2014 the truly universal feature across all anode reactions is oxidation.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The commonest feature of reaction at the anode is that$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "electrons are consumed", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "oxidation is involved", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "ions are reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "electrode dissolves", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$By definition, the anode is where oxidation occurs — species lose electrons there, whether in electrolytic or galvanic cells. This is a universal feature of anode reactions, unlike the other listed options, which don't consistently apply across all anode reactions.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Electrons are actually RELEASED (produced) at the anode during oxidation, not consumed — it's the cathode where electrons are consumed (in reduction). C: Reduction (gaining electrons) happens at the cathode, not the anode — the anode's defining feature is oxidation (losing electrons), the opposite process. D: The electrode dissolving is only true for specific cases (like an active/sacrificial anode), not a universal feature of anode reactions in general — the truly universal feature across all anode reactions is oxidation.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0397$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Rates of Reaction$q$, $q$Activation Energy and Catalysts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Activation Energy and Catalysts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "c4kxyo", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "The potential energy of the reactants is fixed by their chemical identity and doesn't change just because a catalyst provides an alternative pathway.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "The heat of reaction (\u0394H) depends only on the energy difference between reactants and products, not on the pathway between them \u2014 a catalyst changes the pathway (and hence the activation energy) but not this overall energy difference.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Like the reactants, the products' potential energy is fixed by their chemical identity \u2014 a catalyst doesn't alter what the products are or their inherent energy.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following will change when a catalyst is added to a chemical reaction?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "The activation energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "The potential energy of the reactants", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "The heat of reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "The potential energy of the products", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$A catalyst works by providing an alternative reaction pathway with lower activation energy, speeding up the reaction without being consumed. It does NOT change the potential energy of the reactants or products, and it does not change the overall heat of reaction (ΔH) — those are fixed by the identities of reactants and products, independent of the pathway taken.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: The potential energy of the reactants is fixed by their chemical identity and doesn't change just because a catalyst provides an alternative pathway. C: The heat of reaction (ΔH) depends only on the energy difference between reactants and products, not on the pathway between them — a catalyst changes the pathway (and hence the activation energy) but not this overall energy difference. D: Like the reactants, the products' potential energy is fixed by their chemical identity — a catalyst doesn't alter what the products are or their inherent energy.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0398$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Oxidation & Reduction$q$, $q$Oxidizing and Reducing Agents$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Oxidizing and Reducing Agents.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "0wt48x", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "An oxidizing agent's oxidation number DECREASES (it is reduced), not increases \u2014 this has the direction of change backwards for Y's role in the reaction.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This question is about redox (electron transfer), not acid-base (proton transfer) chemistry \u2014 'losing protons' describes a different type of reaction entirely.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Like option C, this frames the reaction in terms of protons rather than electrons \u2014 redox reactions are defined by electron transfer, not proton transfer, so this mismatches the reaction type being described.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$If Y is an oxidizing agent that reacts with a reducing agent, Z, which of the following is correct?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Y increases in oxidation number", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Y becomes reduced", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "Z loses protons", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "Z gains protons", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$By definition, an oxidizing agent is itself reduced during the reaction (it gains electrons, its oxidation number decreases) while causing the other species (the reducing agent) to be oxidised. So Y, the oxidizing agent, becomes reduced.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: An oxidizing agent's oxidation number DECREASES (it is reduced), not increases — this has the direction of change backwards for Y's role in the reaction. C: This question is about redox (electron transfer), not acid-base (proton transfer) chemistry — 'losing protons' describes a different type of reaction entirely. D: Like option C, this frames the reaction in terms of protons rather than electrons — redox reactions are defined by electron transfer, not proton transfer, so this mismatches the reaction type being described.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0399$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Equilibrium$q$, $q$Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Le Chatelier's Principle — Pressure & Temperature Effects.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kzcd9t", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This reaction has 2 moles of reactant gas becoming 3 moles of product gas \u2014 increased pressure would shift it LEFT (toward fewer moles), not right.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Like option A, this has fewer moles of gas as reactant (2) than product (3) \u2014 increasing pressure favours the side with fewer moles, which is the left (reactant) side here, not the right.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This reaction has equal moles of gas on both sides (2 reactant, 2 product) \u2014 a pressure change has no effect on its equilibrium position at all, so it can't shift right due to pressure.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$When at equilibrium, which of the reactions below will shift to the right if the pressure is increased and the temperature is kept constant?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2SO3(g) \u21cc 2SO2(g) + O2(g)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "2CO2(g) \u21cc 2CO(g) + O2(g)", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2H2(g) + O2(g) \u21cc 2H2O(g)", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "2NO(g) \u21cc N2(g) + O2(g)", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Increasing pressure shifts equilibrium toward the side with fewer moles of gas. Only 2H2(g) + O2(g) ⇌ 2H2O(g) has fewer gas moles on the product (right) side — 3 moles of reactant gas becoming 2 moles of product gas — so increased pressure shifts it to the right. The other reactions either have more moles on the product side (shifting left instead) or equal moles on both sides (no pressure effect).$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This reaction has 2 moles of reactant gas becoming 3 moles of product gas — increased pressure would shift it LEFT (toward fewer moles), not right. B: Like option A, this has fewer moles of gas as reactant (2) than product (3) — increasing pressure favours the side with fewer moles, which is the left (reactant) side here, not the right. D: This reaction has equal moles of gas on both sides (2 reactant, 2 product) — a pressure change has no effect on its equilibrium position at all, so it can't shift right due to pressure.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0400$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Electrolysis$q$, $q$Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions — Preferential Discharge$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2003 question on Electrolysis of Aqueous Solutions — Preferential Discharge.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "tqaefy", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 69, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Na+ is never discharged from aqueous solution (it's too reactive) \u2014 this option incorrectly assumes the metal ion itself is deposited, which doesn't happen with inert electrodes in water.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Na+ can't be discharged for the same reason as above; and while OH- discharge does happen in dilute solution, the question specifies a CONCENTRATED solution, where Cl- is preferentially discharged instead.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "H+ at the cathode is correct, but OH- is only preferentially discharged at the anode in dilute solution \u2014 in the concentrated solution specified here, the high Cl- concentration means chloride is discharged instead.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2003, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the electrolysis of a concentrated solution of sodium chloride using inert electrodes, which of the following ions are discharged at the cathode and anode respectively?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Na+ and Cl-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Na+ and OH-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "H+ and OH-", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "H+ and Cl-", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$At the cathode, Na+ is too reactive to be discharged in aqueous solution, so H+ (from water) is preferentially reduced to H2 gas instead. At the anode, although OH- is normally preferentially discharged over Cl- in dilute solution, at high (concentrated) chloride concentration, Cl- is discharged instead, producing Cl2 gas — this is the industrially important chlor-alkali process.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Na+ is never discharged from aqueous solution (it's too reactive) — this option incorrectly assumes the metal ion itself is deposited, which doesn't happen with inert electrodes in water. B: Na+ can't be discharged for the same reason as above; and while OH- discharge does happen in dilute solution, the question specifies a CONCENTRATED solution, where Cl- is preferentially discharged instead. C: H+ at the cathode is correct, but OH- is only preferentially discharged at the anode in dilute solution — in the concentrated solution specified here, the high Cl- concentration means chloride is discharged instead.$q$),
